    "90D" : {
      "localizations" : {
        "de" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "90T"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A day is hard when its training load is in the top %@ of your training days; hard days in a row count once, from the first. Around each, every value is compared with your usual level: the median of the %lld days before it. Averaging over all hard days cancels out what happens by chance and leaves your typical response; the band is its 95%% confidence interval, and a change counts as clear only when the band excludes zero. Rest days, averaged the same way, show what happens without training.\n\nHRV and sleep belong to the night ending on a day, so they show a hard day's effect from the day after. This shows what tends to follow hard training, not what causes it: travel, illness or stress around hard days count too." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "de" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Ein Tag ist hart, wenn seine Trainingsbelastung zu den obersten %1$@ deiner Trainingstage gehört; harte Tage in Folge zählen einmal, ab dem ersten. Um jeden herum wird jeder Wert mit deinem üblichen Niveau verglichen: dem Median der %2$lld Tage davor. Der Mittelwert über alle harten Tage hebt Zufälle auf und lässt deine typische Reaktion übrig; das Band ist ihr 95-%%-Konfidenzintervall, und eine Änderung gilt nur dann als klar, wenn das Band die Null ausschließt. Ruhetage, ebenso gemittelt, zeigen, was ohne Training geschieht.\n\nHFV und Schlaf gehören zur Nacht, die an einem Tag endet, daher zeigen sie die Wirkung eines harten Tages ab dem Tag danach. Das zeigt, was hartem Training meist folgt, nicht, was es verursacht: Reisen, Krankheit oder Stress rund um harte Tage zählen mit."
          }
        },
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "A day is hard when its training load is in the top %1$@ of your training days; hard days in a row count once, from the first. Around each, every value is compared with your usual level: the median of the %2$lld days before it. Averaging over all hard days cancels out what happens by chance and leaves your typical response; the band is its 95%% confidence interval, and a change counts as clear only when the band excludes zero. Rest days, averaged the same way, show what happens without training.\n\nHRV and sleep belong to the night ending on a day, so they show a hard day's effect from the day after. This shows what tends to follow hard training, not what causes it: travel, illness or stress around hard days count too."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A personal, open-source project. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Garmin or Wahoo Fitness. Garmin and Garmin Connect are trademarks of Garmin Ltd." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "de" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "translated",
            "value" : "Ein persönliches Open-Source-Projekt, weder mit Garmin noch mit Wahoo Fitness verbunden oder von ihnen unterstützt. Garmin und Garmin Connect sind Marken von Garmin Ltd."
          }
        }
      }
    },
